facts:
A line sloping **up** shows a quantity increasing at a constant rate; a line sloping **down** shows one decreasing. A decreasing line has a negative gradient, but its **rate of decrease** is the positive magnitude of that gradient.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0.2,0.5)--(3.2,2.9);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Quantity};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Sloping up: increasing};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=6.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.5,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0.2,2.9)--(3.2,0.5);
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.6) {Quantity};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-0.55) {Time};
\node[font=\small] at (1.7,-1.25) {Sloping down: decreasing};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

When graphs use the **same axis scales and units**, the **steeper** line has the greater magnitude of rate: the quantity changes more quickly. Greater steepness does not necessarily mean a greater signed gradient; a steeper downward line has a more negative gradient.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.2,0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0.3)--(2.6,3.0);
\draw[thick,blue] (0,0.3)--(4.0,1.6);
\node[font=\scriptsize,above right] at (2.6,3.0) {Steeper: greater rate magnitude};
\node[font=\scriptsize,right] at (4.0,1.6) {Less steep: smaller rate magnitude};
\node[rotate=90,font=\small] at (-0.5,1.7) {Quantity};
\node[font=\small] at (2.1,-0.55) {Time};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

